  * = |B/C - 1| <= the parallel-branch tolerance. On a SUBJECT row the fit DISCARDS the memory branch
  there and the cell yields NO alpha whatever the tread column says, because two branches within 15% of
  each other are one line. On the BLOCK_M=256 REFERENCE row it is harmless: that ladder is never fitted
  for a memory branch, only for proportionality, and 0/m treads is what qualifies it.
  treads n/m = memory-bound treads out of the ladder's length. 0/m is a compute reference; m/m is a cell
  whose alpha needs no reference for membership. The B/C columns are ANCHORED at 0.991, the measured median over the 22
  published BLOCK_M=128 ladders, so the kernel's achieved rho cancels. The last two columns are the same
  quantity computed from the CALIBRATED ridge instead; they run about 15% high, and taken literally they say
  the BLOCK_M=256 reference at BN=64 is memory bound -- which it measurably is not, in 22 of 24 published arms.

## The plan

model        mixtral-8x7b E=8 k=2 bf16
pinned       {'BLOCK_SIZE_K': 64, 'GROUP_SIZE_M': 16, 'num_warps': 8, 'num_stages': 4}  (BLOCK_SIZE_N is the sweep)
BN grid      [32, 64, 128]
subjects     [16, 32, 64, 128], primary 128; reference 256. EVERY arm's reference is measured first, across all arms, and level-checked before any subject costs anything -- an arm that qualifies none of its own can still borrow one, and whether it can is not knowable until the others exist
repeats      17 round-robin passes per setting
identification  corr(g1, 1/BN) over this design's 12 cells = 0.552. g1 is the three-term model's only BLOCK_N-dependent
             column and is strictly proportional to BLOCK_M; 1/BN is the rival that fits the 2026-09-10 arm 3.1x
             better at equal parameter count. Where the two are collinear no repeat count separates them.
             without BLOCK_M=16 the same number is 0.663 over 9 cells, which is what the fourth height is worth.
             the committed 2026-09-10 arm yielded 0 of 3 BLOCK_M=128 cells with an alpha, so the fit registered on 3 heights was made over 2: [32, 64].
             BLOCK_M=16 measured at BLOCK_N=64, GROUP_SIZE_M=1, num_warps=8, num_stages=4: 56 of 56 treads memory bound at B/C = 5.46, against the parallel-branch tolerance |B/C - 1| <= 0.15 that discarded every BLOCK_M=128 cell in the committed bn arm. 9 of 9 gates PASS.
               source: /workspace/moe-kernels/results/published/2026-09-10-nvidia_h200-gaps-session/results/tile_cap/nvidia_h200-bm16-budget400.0-ctl256-dtypebf16-g1-iters50-l2flushtrue-modelmixtral_8x7b-n64-plant-ce661a4a/report.json
design power at GROUP_SIZE_M=16: the corpus puts alpha_b near 0.61 there, and the response moves with alpha_a as g1 (1 - alpha_b)/(1 + phi)^2, so the lever is 100% of its size at GROUP_SIZE_M=16.
             planted TRUTH world at GROUP_SIZE_M=16, 17 reps, spread 1.82% (the worst published across-repeat spread (H200 s4 ladders 0.76-1.82%, A100 0.48-0.61%); nothing measured was available to resolve it from): sd(alpha_a) = 0.1675 against the 0.025 C1 needs -> CANNOT RESOLVE alpha_a
             that sd is the MEDIAN over 5 planted realisations, which ran 0.0941, 0.0959, 0.1675, 0.2271, 0.2529; the verdict is scored on the WORST of them (0.2529). One realisation is not a property of the design: at the worst published spread this figure swings by an order of magnitude between seeds, and it is what decides whether the arm is paid for.
             AT THIS PINNING C1 WILL READ UNKNOWN however the run goes. --group-m 16 is the setting that resolves it; --reps buys the rest, and this line is what says how much it bought.
             C6 will FAIL with it and both are CLAIM gates, so the arm is expected to end 1 CLAIM_FAIL, printed as exit 0 without --fail-on-gate. It is NOT 3 INVALID and must not be re-measured: alpha_b, C3 and C5 are what a run at this pinning is for and they stay quotable.
             MDE (alpha_a, planted TRUTH world, two-sided 5% at 80% power): 0.4691 in alpha_a, from sd 0.1675. Assumption: lognormal spread 1.82% on every timing, from the worst published across-repeat spread (H200 s4 ladders 0.76-1.82%, A100 0.48-0.61%); nothing measured was available to resolve it from; 200 bootstrap draws. Beside it, the cross-arm floor 0.0229 ASSUMED (s3-vs-s4 paired sd 0.0323 over 11 cells / sqrt(2). CONFOUNDS num_stages with rerun noise, so it is an upper bound on SAME-SESSION rerun noise and on nothing else: both arms ran in the same pod hour (/workspace/session/20260901T214218Z), so it is NOT established for cross-pod, cross-day or cross-card comparisons. It is also heteroscedastic: mixtral n=4 sd 0.0486 against qwen2 n=7 sd 0.0186, a 2.61x ratio, so it must not be quoted per cell at mixtral G>=8 [no measured floor: results/published/NOISE_FLOOR.json carries stages_control but replicate_floor is null: part (a) has not run on a card yet]): this run's own bootstrap resamples WITHIN-PROCESS warm repeats and cannot see anything that changes between processes, so it is a lower bound and the floor is the DECLARED upper one and not a measurement
timing       300 ms of warmup under load, then 3 trial(s) of 400 ms of kernel time each, L2 flush ON; the instrument sizes its own iteration count from the warmup's queue-deep per-call time
timings      1836 (108 treads x 17 reps)
estimate     2754 s of GPU at what the instrument charges (warmup + trials x budget per timing), excluding compiles and allocation
